        <item rdf:about="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41567-026-03434-w">
            <title>[7] Electric fields take control of ferrons</title>
            <link>https://www.nature.com/articles/s41567-026-03434-w</link>
            <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><b>Last authors:</b> Peiran Tong, He Tian</p><p><span style='display:inline-block;padding:4px 9px;border-radius:999px;background:#fee2e2;color:#991b1b;font-weight:700;'>Score 7/10</span> <b>B_IMPORTANT_CONDMAT</b></p><p><b>Why:</b> Condensed matter paper on controlling collective excitations (ferrons) with electric fields.</p><p><b>Tags:</b> <span style='display:inline-block;margin:2px 4px 2px 0;padding:2px 6px;border-radius:999px;background:#eef2ff;color:#3730a3;font-size:12px;'>#condensedmatter</span> <span style='display:inline-block;margin:2px 4px 2px 0;padding:2px 6px;border-radius:999px;background:#eef2ff;color:#3730a3;font-size:12px;'>#quasiparticles</span> <span style='display:inline-block;margin:2px 4px 2px 0;padding:2px 6px;border-radius:999px;background:#eef2ff;color:#3730a3;font-size:12px;'>#electricfields</span></p><p><img src='https://media.springernature.com/m685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41567-026-03434-w/MediaObjects/41567_2026_3434_Fig1_HTML.png' style='max-width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px;' /></p><hr/><p><b>Abstract:</b> Ferrons are the quasiparticles associated with the collective excitations of spontaneous polarization. External electric fields have now been shown to tune their properties in a nonvolatile manner.</p>]]></content:encoded>
            <dc:title>[7] Electric fields take control of ferrons</dc:title>
            <dc:creator>Peiran Tong, He Tian</dc:creator><dc:creator>He Tian</dc:creator>
            <dc:identifier>doi:10.1038/s41567-026-03434-w</dc:identifier>
            <dc:source>Nature Physics, Published online: 2026-09-10; | doi:10.1038/s41567-026-03434-w</dc:source>
            <dc:date>2026-09-10</dc:date>
            <prism:publicationName>Nature Physics</prism:publicationName>
            <prism:doi>10.1038/s41567-026-03434-w</prism:doi>
            <prism:url>https://www.nature.com/articles/s41567-026-03434-w</prism:url>
        <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Last authors:&lt;/b&gt; Peiran Tong, He Tian&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style='display:inline-block;padding:4px 9px;border-radius:999px;background:#fee2e2;color:#991b1b;font-weight:700;'&gt;Score 7/10&lt;/span&gt; &lt;b&gt;B_IMPORTANT_CONDMAT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Why:&lt;/b&gt; Condensed matter paper on controlling collective excitations (ferrons) with electric fields.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Tags:&lt;/b&gt; &lt;span style='display:inline-block;margin:2px 4px 2px 0;padding:2px 6px;border-radius:999px;background:#eef2ff;color:#3730a3;font-size:12px;'&gt;#condensedmatter&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style='display:inline-block;margin:2px 4px 2px 0;padding:2px 6px;border-radius:999px;background:#eef2ff;color:#3730a3;font-size:12px;'&gt;#quasiparticles&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style='display:inline-block;margin:2px 4px 2px 0;padding:2px 6px;border-radius:999px;background:#eef2ff;color:#3730a3;font-size:12px;'&gt;#electricfields&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src='https://media.springernature.com/m685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41567-026-03434-w/MediaObjects/41567_2026_3434_Fig1_HTML.png' style='max-width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px;' /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Abstract:&lt;/b&gt; Ferrons are the quasiparticles associated with the collective excitations of spontaneous polarization. External electric fields have now been shown to tune their properties in a nonvolatile manner.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item>
    
        <item rdf:about="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41567-026-03444-8">
            <title>[7] Trion Hall effect in electron–hole double layers</title>
            <link>https://www.nature.com/articles/s41567-026-03444-8</link>
            <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><b>Last authors:</b> Kin Fai Mak, Jie Shan</p><p><b>Authors:</b> Raghav Chaturvedi, Phuong X. Nguyen, Patrick Knüppel, et al.</p><p><span style='display:inline-block;padding:4px 9px;border-radius:999px;background:#fee2e2;color:#991b1b;font-weight:700;'>Score 7/10</span> <b>B_IMPORTANT_CONDMAT</b></p><p><b>Why:</b> Experimental demonstration of Trion Hall effect in electron-hole double layers.</p><p><b>Tags:</b> <span style='display:inline-block;margin:2px 4px 2px 0;padding:2px 6px;border-radius:999px;background:#eef2ff;color:#3730a3;font-size:12px;'>#Halleffect</span> <span style='display:inline-block;margin:2px 4px 2px 0;padding:2px 6px;border-radius:999px;background:#eef2ff;color:#3730a3;font-size:12px;'>#trions</span> <span style='display:inline-block;margin:2px 4px 2px 0;padding:2px 6px;border-radius:999px;background:#eef2ff;color:#3730a3;font-size:12px;'>#2Dmaterials</span> <span style='display:inline-block;margin:2px 4px 2px 0;padding:2px 6px;border-radius:999px;background:#eef2ff;color:#3730a3;font-size:12px;'>#electron-holedoublelayers</span></p><p><img src='https://media.springernature.com/m685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41567-026-03444-8/MediaObjects/41567_2026_3444_Fig1_HTML.png' style='max-width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px;' /></p><hr/><p><b>Abstract:</b> A trion fluid—which forms when excitons bind to an extra electron or hole—is expected to exhibit a Hall effect in magnetic fields. Now, this is demonstrated in electron–hole double layers.</p>]]></content:encoded>
            <dc:title>[7] Trion Hall effect in electron–hole double layers</dc:title>
            <dc:creator>Last authors: Kin Fai Mak, Jie Shan; Authors: Raghav Chaturvedi, Phuong X. Nguyen, Patrick Knüppel, et al.</dc:creator><dc:creator>Phuong X. Nguyen</dc:creator><dc:creator>Patrick Knüppel</dc:creator><dc:creator>Kenji Watanabe</dc:creator><dc:creator>Takashi Taniguchi</dc:creator><dc:creator>Kin Fai Mak</dc:creator><dc:creator>Jie Shan</dc:creator>
            <dc:identifier>doi:10.1038/s41567-026-03444-8</dc:identifier>
            <dc:source>Nature Physics, Published online: 2026-09-10; | doi:10.1038/s41567-026-03444-8</dc:source>
            <dc:date>2026-09-10</dc:date>
            <prism:publicationName>Nature Physics</prism:publicationName>
            <prism:doi>10.1038/s41567-026-03444-8</prism:doi>
            <prism:url>https://www.nature.com/articles/s41567-026-03444-8</prism:url>
        <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Last authors:&lt;/b&gt; Kin Fai Mak, Jie Shan&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Authors:&lt;/b&gt; Raghav Chaturvedi, Phuong X. Nguyen, Patrick Knüppel, et al.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style='display:inline-block;padding:4px 9px;border-radius:999px;background:#fee2e2;color:#991b1b;font-weight:700;'&gt;Score 7/10&lt;/span&gt; &lt;b&gt;B_IMPORTANT_CONDMAT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Why:&lt;/b&gt; Experimental demonstration of Trion Hall effect in electron-hole double layers.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Tags:&lt;/b&gt; &lt;span style='display:inline-block;margin:2px 4px 2px 0;padding:2px 6px;border-radius:999px;background:#eef2ff;color:#3730a3;font-size:12px;'&gt;#Halleffect&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style='display:inline-block;margin:2px 4px 2px 0;padding:2px 6px;border-radius:999px;background:#eef2ff;color:#3730a3;font-size:12px;'&gt;#trions&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style='display:inline-block;margin:2px 4px 2px 0;padding:2px 6px;border-radius:999px;background:#eef2ff;color:#3730a3;font-size:12px;'&gt;#2Dmaterials&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style='display:inline-block;margin:2px 4px 2px 0;padding:2px 6px;border-radius:999px;background:#eef2ff;color:#3730a3;font-size:12px;'&gt;#electron-holedoublelayers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src='https://media.springernature.com/m685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41567-026-03444-8/MediaObjects/41567_2026_3444_Fig1_HTML.png' style='max-width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px;' /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Abstract:&lt;/b&gt; A trion fluid—which forms when excitons bind to an extra electron or hole—is expected to exhibit a Hall effect in magnetic fields. Now, this is demonstrated in electron–hole double layers.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item>
    
